IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `fca-nouns`
IMPORT `fca-definitions`
IMPORT `fca-s3-application`
IMPORT `fca-s2-adjustment`

-- Frustrated Contracts Act 1959 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation -- LAYER 1, THE COURT'S POWERS AND DUTIES
-- AS REGULATIVE RULES.
--
-- The Act names one actor who does anything: "the court".  Its permissions (s 2(3), (5), (7)), its prohibition (s 2(6)) and its duties
-- (s 3(3), (4)) are written here as PARTY ... MAY / SHANT / MUST rules, each guarded by the decision function in the section modules
-- (so the two cannot disagree).  There is NO seat for the court: these rules are here for what they mean to the party before the court.
--
-- The Act states NO time limit and NO consequence for any of them, so none is invented: no WITHIN, no HENCE beyond FULFILLED, no LEST.
-- Consequently a MUST with no deadline never reaches BREACH in a trace, and an unused MAY stays open; that is the text, not a gap in the encoding.
-- The outcome of a DEONTIC value cannot be compared in an #ASSERT on this binary, so the traces in fca-tests-court.l4 are RUN and their
-- printed outcomes recorded in NOTES.md; what is ASSERTED is the guard (the decision function) beside each trace.
-- The same permissions, prohibition and duties exist as decision functions over the facts (the "duty arises" / "may" functions), as the brief asks.

§ `The actor and the acts`

DECLARE `A party under the Frustrated Contracts Act` IS ONE OF
    `the court`

DECLARE `An act under the Frustrated Contracts Act` IS ONE OF
    `allow the party to retain or recover the whole or part of the sums`
    `include a reasonable sum for overhead expenses and for work performed personally`
    `treat the benefit as a benefit obtained by the person who assumed the obligations`
    `take the insurance sums into account`
    `give effect to the provision of the contract`
    `treat the part of the contract as a separate contract that has not been frustrated`

§ `The court's permissions -- section 2`

-- src:49 | (3) If the party to whom the sums were so paid or payable under
-- src:50 | subsection (2) incurred expenses before the time of discharge in, or
-- src:51 | for the purpose of, the performance of the contract, the court may, if it
-- src:52 | considers it just to do so having regard to all the circumstances of the
-- src:53 | case, allow him to retain or, as the case may be, recover the whole or
@ref s 2(3) (src:49-53)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      sum IS A `Sum facts`
      exp IS AN `Expenses facts`
      `the court considers it just to allow the party to retain or recover the sum, having regard to all the circumstances of the case` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under the Frustrated Contracts Act` `An act under the Frustrated Contracts Act`
`s 2(3) the court's permission to allow the party to retain or recover` MEANS
    IF `s 2(3) the court may allow the party to retain or recover the whole or part of the sums` c sum exp `the court considers it just to allow the party to retain or recover the sum, having regard to all the circumstances of the case` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the court` MAY `allow the party to retain or recover the whole or part of the sums`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- src:82 | (5) In estimating, for the purposes of this section, the amount of any
-- src:83 | expenses incurred by any party to the contract, the court may, without
-- src:84 | prejudice to the generality of this section, include such sum as
@ref s 2(5) (src:82-84)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      exp IS AN `Expenses facts`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under the Frustrated Contracts Act` `An act under the Frustrated Contracts Act`
`s 2(5) the court's permission to include overhead expenses and personal work` MEANS
    IF `s 2(5) the court may include a reasonable sum for overhead expenses and for work performed personally` c exp EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the court` MAY `include a reasonable sum for overhead expenses and for work performed personally`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- src:96 | (7) Where any person has assumed obligations under the contract in
-- src:97 | consideration of the conferring of a benefit by any other party to the
-- src:98 | contract upon any other person, whether a party to the contract or not,
-- src:99 | the court may, if in all the circumstances of the case it considers it just
@ref s 2(7) (src:96-99)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      o IS AN `Obligor facts`
      `the court considers it just, in all the circumstances of the case, to treat the benefit as obtained by the person who assumed the obligations` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under the Frustrated Contracts Act` `An act under the Frustrated Contracts Act`
`s 2(7) the court's permission to treat the benefit as obtained by the person who assumed the obligations` MEANS
    IF `s 2(7) the court may treat the benefit conferred on another person as a benefit obtained by the person who assumed the obligations` c o `the court considers it just, in all the circumstances of the case, to treat the benefit as obtained by the person who assumed the obligations` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the court` MAY `treat the benefit as a benefit obtained by the person who assumed the obligations`
    ELSE FULFILLED

§ `The court's prohibition -- section 2(6)`

-- The prohibition is derived from the decision function (ruling N2 of 2026-11-04: a flat negative counts as the text's no, and where the text names
-- the bearer, the prohibition is derived from that answer).  The "unless" lifts it: where an obligation to insure existed the guard is JUST FALSE and nothing is prohibited.
-- src:88 | (6) In considering whether any sum ought to be recovered or
-- src:89 | retained under this section by any party to the contract, the court shall
-- src:90 | not take into account any sums which have, by reason of the
-- src:91 | circumstances giving rise to the frustration of the contract, become
@ref s 2(6) (src:88-91)
GIVEN i IS AN `Insurance facts`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under the Frustrated Contracts Act` `An act under the Frustrated Contracts Act`
`s 2(6) the court's prohibition on taking the insurance sums into account` MEANS
    IF `s 2(6) the court shall not take the insurance sums into account` i EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the court` SHANT `take the insurance sums into account`
    ELSE FULFILLED

§ `The court's duties -- section 3`

-- src:116 | (3) Where any contract to which this Act applies contains any
-- src:117 | provision which, upon the true construction of the contract, is
-- src:118 | intended to have effect in the event of circumstances arising which
-- src:119 | operate, or would but for that provision operate, to frustrate the
-- src:120 | contract, or is intended to have effect whether such circumstances
-- src:121 | arise or not, the court shall give effect to that provision and shall only
@ref s 3(3) (src:116-121)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      p IS A `Contract provision facts`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under the Frustrated Contracts Act` `An act under the Frustrated Contracts Act`
`s 3(3) the court's duty to give effect to the provision of the contract` MEANS
    IF `s 3(3) the court is to give effect to the provision of the contract` c p EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the court` MUST `give effect to the provision of the contract`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- src:125 | (4) Where it appears to the court that a part of any contract to which
-- src:126 | this Act applies can properly be severed from the remainder of the
-- src:127 | contract, being a part wholly performed before the time of discharge,
-- src:128 | or so performed except for the payment in respect of that part of the
-- src:129 | contract of sums which are or can be ascertained under the contract,
-- src:130 | the court shall treat that part of the contract as if it were a separate
-- src:131 | contract and had not been frustrated and shall treat section 2 as only
@ref s 3(4) (src:125-131)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      s IS A `Severable part facts`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `A party under the Frustrated Contracts Act` `An act under the Frustrated Contracts Act`
`s 3(4) the court's duty to treat the part of the contract as a separate contract` MEANS
    IF `s 3(4) the court is to treat the part of the contract as a separate contract that has not been frustrated` c s EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the court` MUST `treat the part of the contract as a separate contract that has not been frustrated`
    ELSE FULFILLED
